    "current_example": {
      "math_academy_id": "e-21657",
      "problem": "Consider the system of linear differential equations below.\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=9x(t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe eigenvalues of the system's matrix are $\\lambda_{1,2} = \\pm3\\text{i},$ for which the corresponding eigenvectors are $\\mathbf{v}_{1,2} = [\\pm \\text{i}, 3]^T,$ respectively. Given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14130022/assets/1f353664beb7c32a70985ed2195e46d88d1120af8a5d1c76d4ed195f452ccb83.svg)\nsketch the phase portrait of the system of differential equations given above.",
      "worked_solution": "We are given that the general solution of the system is\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14130022/assets/d63532f1849bee96c896a799f3a4d870d33e1bf403eae6148fc22734ec90efdd.svg)\n\nFor example, when $c_1=1$ and $c_2=0,$ we obtain the following curve:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}x=-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(3t) \\\\ y=3\\operatorname{cos}⁡(3t)\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the coordinates (rounded to two decimal places) of several points on the curve, we get the table below.\n\n| $t$ | $0$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{12}$ | $\\dfrac{\\pi}{6}$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $x$ | $0$ | $-0.71$ | $-1$ |\n| $y$ | $3$ | $2.12$ | $0$ |\n\nLet's sketch this solution in the phase space.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14130022/assets/3c2ea4467854f749e07f5ae3922273a2ebd441da4fb9df834ce0676157e856a2.png)\n\nNotice that $|\\sin(3t)| \\leq 1$ and $|\\cos(3t)| \\leq 1.$ Thus, the obtained curve is bounded. So, we have an ellipse that is traversed counterclockwise.\n\nThe phase portrait of our system looks as follows:\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/capture/multivariable/14130022/assets/df8e7ae5f7ff7281cbdbf5849dc94faf59d2421621dc5009b21eeb947485d153.png)",
